([nil nil ((#("* Chemical thermodynamics
One of the innovations enabling modern automotive emissions control was the introduction of an ``oxygen storage'' material to the catalytic converter. The function of the material is to buffer variations in oxygen pressure in the engine exhaust, so the three-way exhaust catalyst works more effectively. Doped ceria is the most common oxygen storage material, and its equilibrium reaction with \\ce{O2} can be written:

#+BEGIN_CENTER
\\ce{Ce2O3 (s) + 1/2 O2 (g) <=> 2 CeO2 (s)}
#+END_CENTER
\\noindent The following data were obtained for the equilibrium \\ce{O2} pressure above a mixture of solid \\ce{Ce2O3} (MW \\SI{328}{g/mol}) and \\ce{CeO2} (\\SI{172}{g/mol}).
|-------+--------------------+-------------------------------|
| T (K) | $P_{\\ce{O2}}$ (Pa) | $\\ln P_{\\ce{O2}}/1\\text{ Pa}$ |
|  <c>  |        <c>         |              <c>              |
|-------+--------------------+-------------------------------|
|  800  |       0.0066       |            $-5.0$             |
|  900  |       0.2215       |            $-1.5$             |
|-------+--------------------+-------------------------------|
**  (30 pts) Determine the *standard state enthalpy*, *standard state entropy*, and *standard state Gibbs free energy* of reaction at \\SI{900}{K}. Be sure to indicate your choice of standard state and any approximations you use. You can assume that \\ce{O2} is ideal at these conditions and that the solids are phase-pure.

*** Answer
1. (10 pts) As solid substances, the activities of \\ce{Ce2O3} and \\ce{CeO2} are unity. Take Pa as standard state pressure.

 \\[\\Delta G^\\circ(900) = - R (\\SI{900}{K}) \\ln K_{eq} = - R (\\SI{900}{K}) \\ln P_{\\ce{O2}}^{-1/2} = 1/2 R (\\SI{900}{K}) (-1.5) \\]

 \\[\\Delta G^\\circ(900) = \\SI{-5.6}{kJ/mol} \\]

2. (10 pts) Two temperatures available. Use van't Hoff equation to infer $\\Delta H$:
 \\[\\frac{\\ln K(900)}{\\ln K(800)} \\approx \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ}{R}\\left (\\frac{1}{900} - \\frac{1}{800} \\right )\\]

 \\[\\frac{-1/2 (-1.5)}{-1/2 (-5.0)} = \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ}{R}\\left (\\frac{1}{900} - \\frac{1}{800} \\right )\\]

 \\[\\Delta H^\\circ \\approx \\SI{-18.0}{kJ/mol} \\]

3. (10 pts) Use \\(\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T \\Delta S\\) to infer \\(\\Delta S\\):

  \\[ \\Delta S^\\circ = \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ - \\Delta G^\\circ(900)}{\\SI{900}{K}} \\]

  \\[ \\Delta S^\\circ \\approx \\SI{-14}{J/mol K} \\]

\\noindent \\( \\Delta S < 0 \\). That's reassuring.
\\newpage
**  (10 pts) Determine the standard state free energy of reaction at \\SI{900}{K}. Be sure to indicate your choice of standard state. You can assume that \\ce{O2} is ideal at these conditions and that the solids are phase-pure. :noexport:
\\vspace{6cm}

*** answer :noexport:
K = 1/PO2^1/2  Delta G0 = -RT ln K
** (10 pts) Estimate the standard state enthalpy of reaction. Be sure to indicate units and any assumptions. :noexport:
\\newpage
** (10 pts) Estimate the standard state entropy of reaction. Be sure to indicate units and any assumptions. :noexport:
\\vspace{7cm}
** (10 pts) Make a rough sketch of the chemical potentials of \\ce{Ce2O3} and \\ce{CeO2} vs \\(\\mu_{\\ce{O2}}\\) at \\SI{627}{\\celsius}. :noexport:
\\newpage
** Suppose a closed, constant volume vessel contains \\SI{50}{g} of \\ce{Ce2O3} and \\SI{50}{g} of \\ce{CeO2} in equilibrium with \\ce{O2} at \\SI{900}{K}. You introduce \\SI{0.32}{g} \\ce{O2} into the vessel. After the vessel returns to equilibrium:
*** (10 pts) What is the pressure of \\ce{O2} in the vessel? 
1. If the volume and temperature haven't changed, then the equilibrium pressure must be the same. \\SI{0.2215}{Pa}.
*** (10 pts) What are the masses of \\ce{Ce2O3} and \\ce{CeO2} in the vessel?
1. Added \\( \\SI{0.32}{g} = \\SI{0.01}{mol} \\ce{O2}\\). From stoichiometry, to consume all, must lose \\SI{0.02}{mol} \\ce{Ce2O3} and gain \\SI{0.04}{mol} \\ce{CeO2}:

 \\[ m_{\\ce{Ce2O3}} = \\SI{50}{g} - \\SI{0.02}{mol} \\times \\SI{328}{g/mol} = \\SI{43.4}{g} \\]

 \\[ m_{\\ce{CeO2}} = \\SI{50}{g} + \\SI{0.04}{mol} \\times \\SI{172}{g/mol} = \\SI{56.9}{g} \\]

