* Kinetics
The acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of a cobalt fluoride complex was followed spectrophotometrically to obtain reaction lifetimes:
#+BEGIN_CENTER
\\ce{Co(NH3)5F^2+ (aq) + H2O ->[H+] Co(NH3)5(OH)^3+ (aq) + F- (aq)}
#+END_CENTER

|-----------------------+-------------+-------------------+-------------------|
| \\ce{Co(NH3)5F^2+} (M) | \\ce{H+} (M) | \\(t_{1/2} \\) (hr) | \\(t_{1/4} \\) (hr) |
|          <c>          |     <c>     |        <c>        |        <c>        |
|-----------------------+-------------+-------------------+-------------------|
|         $0.1$         |   $0.01$    |         1         |         2         |
|         $0.2$         |   $0.01$    |         1         |         2         |
|         $0.1$         |   $0.02$    |       $0.5$       |         1         |
|-----------------------+-------------+-------------------+-------------------|
** (50 pts) Provide as much information as you can about the reaction rate law based on the available data.

1. (10 pts) \\ce{H2O} in large excess. No information about its contribution to rate. \\ce{H+} is a catalyst. Concentration doesn't change. In absence of other information, assume products don't influence rate. Postulate

 \\[ r = k C_{\\ce{CoF^2+}}^\\alpha C_{\\ce{H+}}^\\beta \\]

2. (15 pts) From first and second experiments, doubling \\(C_\\ce{CoF^2+}\\) at constant \\(C_\\ce{H+}\\) does not change half-life. Must have \\(\\alpha = 1\\).

 \\[ r = k C_{\\ce{CoF^2+}}^1 C_{\\ce{H+}}^\\beta \\]

3. (15 pts) From first and third experiments, doubling \\ce{H+} at constant \\ce{CoF^2+} halves half-life. Because  \\(C_\\ce{H+}\\) is a constant:

 \\[t_{1/2} = \\frac{\\ln 2}{k C_{\\ce{H+}}^\\beta} \\]

 \\[ \\beta = 1 \\]

  \\[ r = k C_{\\ce{CoF^2+}}^1 C_{\\ce{H+}}^1 \\]

4. (10 pts) Can estimate \\(k\\) from any one of the experiments.

 \\[t_{1/2} = \\frac{\\ln 2}{k C_{\\ce{H+}}} \\]

 \\[k = \\frac{\\ln 2}{t_{1/2} C_{\\ce{H+}}} = \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\SI{1}{hr} \\times \\SI{0.01}{M}} = \\SI{69}{/mol /hr} \\]
